nswer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-124816" :question/id #uuid "249b4f98-ecec-5db6-9d63-40905e020bb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-124816" :question/is-example false :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932638/assets/3687e362fea73defca43332ee70f232282ad1eded9dc14cbbc045de4324067f0.png)\n\nThe point $P$ is located on the unit circle, as shown above. The $y$-coordinate of $P$ is $-\\frac{6}{7}.$ What is the $x$-coordinate of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $P$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-\\frac{6}{7}$ into the above and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(-\\frac{6}{7})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(\\frac{36}{49}) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1-\\frac{36}{49} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{13}{49} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{13}{49}} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $x$ is positive in the fourth quadrant. So our final answer is $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-124816/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "021e3349-c54d-55a8-9095-902bba933660"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-124816"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-124822/selection/aa8010f9f552f5eabd22936cfa25d02b6f7cefda1f2cf2ed2f36d19971c3289f" :answer/id #uuid "e41ad4ec-c579-57b1-8e76-eac339371a41" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-\\frac{4}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124822/selection/bd4773417b5390caa73a826ec86613ed139d8bf091cff3b8f9356adabba89283" :answer/id #uuid "ab6d02a8-430a-5997-a791-d23c3e51a078" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124822/selection/f8d77f68e4fd2ba1427c69f3f00adcdb22c35d3c2a693b3092f1c37c36805409" :answer/